Linear Operators
• Linear operators can be defined on both finite and infinite-dimensional vector spaces.
• Linear operator defined on finite-dimensional vector spaces has matrix representations.
Let (X , F) and (Y, F) be the n and m-dimensional vector spaces, respectively, over same field. Let x1, x2, . . . , xn be the set of l.i. vectors in X . Then, the linear operator L : (X , F) → (Y, F) is uniquely determined by the n pairs of mappings yi = Lxi∀ i = 1, 2, . . . , n.
Furthermore, with respect to the basis {x1, x2, . . . , xn} of X , and a basis {u1, u2, . . . , um} of Y, L can be represented by an m × n matrix A with the coefficients in F . The ith column of A is the representation of yi w.r.t. the basis {u1, u2, . . . , um}.
